= Computation =

== Overview ==
'''Computation''' is the rule-governed transformation of information, state, or representations. In everyday computing, it is what happens when a computer follows instructions to turn inputs into results. In theoretical computer science, the idea is broader: computation can be studied independently of any particular machine by describing an abstract model and the operations that model can perform.

Computation is the substrate beneath [[Algorithm|algorithms]], [[Program (computing)|programs]], programming languages, software, data processing, simulation, artificial intelligence, and digital communication.

== Definition ==
A computation is an execution of operations according to some computational model. It normally involves a state, permitted operations or transitions, and rules determining how one state may lead to another.

NIST's Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures describes a '''model of computation''' as a formal abstract definition of a computer used to analyze what can be computed and what resources a computation requires. Models include Turing machines, random-access machines, finite-state machines, cellular automata, parallel models, and quantum models.

== Scope & importance ==
Computation should be distinguished from several nearby ideas:

* An '''algorithm''' is a method or procedure for achieving a result.
* A '''program''' expresses instructions in a form intended for execution by a computing system.
* A '''computation''' is the process or sequence of state changes produced when computational rules are carried out.
* A '''computer''' is a physical or virtual system capable of carrying out computations.

The distinction matters because the same algorithm can be expressed by different programs and executed by different machines while still representing essentially the same computation at an appropriate level of abstraction.

== Core concepts ==
A useful model of computation identifies some combination of:

* '''Representation''' — how numbers, symbols, objects, instructions, or other information are encoded.
* '''State''' — the information needed to describe the system at a particular point.
* '''Operations''' — the transformations the system is allowed to perform.
* '''Transition rules''' — how one state can become another.
* '''Input and output''' — what is supplied to the computation and what result or observable behavior it produces.
* '''Control''' — how the next operation is selected, including sequential, branching, concurrent, probabilistic, or interactive behavior.
* '''Resources''' — time, memory, communication, energy, or other quantities consumed by the computation.

Different computational models expose different details. A finite-state machine emphasizes states and transitions; a random-access machine resembles an idealized conventional computer; a Turing machine is central to computability theory; quantum models use operations permitted by quantum mechanics.

== Examples ==
Adding two integers is a computation: the inputs are numbers, operations implement arithmetic rules, and the output is their sum.

Executing a sorting program is a computation in which a sequence of values is repeatedly transformed until it satisfies an ordering relation.

Rendering a simulated physical system is a computation that repeatedly updates a model state according to numerical rules.

A web server also performs computations when it parses a request, retrieves or transforms data, applies application logic, and constructs a response.

== Computability and complexity ==
Two foundational questions are different:

# '''Computability''' asks whether a problem can be solved by an algorithm under a specified model of computation.
# '''Complexity''' asks how many resources are required to solve it.

NIST defines a computable function as one that can be computed by an algorithm, equivalently by a Turing machine under the classical model. A problem can therefore be computable in principle while still requiring impractical amounts of time or memory for large inputs.

This separation leads toward computability theory, computational complexity, algorithm analysis, and the study of tractable versus intractable problems.

== Applications / implications ==
Computation is not confined to desktop computers. It appears in embedded controllers, phones, networks, databases, robots, scientific instruments, distributed systems, graphics processors, cloud systems, and specialized accelerators.

Computer science also studies computation as an abstract phenomenon. This allows properties such as expressiveness, equivalence, correctness, resource use, and limits to be investigated without depending on one hardware platform or programming language.

== Technical depth ==
A computation can be represented as a sequence or graph of configurations produced by a transition relation. In deterministic models, a state and input determine the next state. In nondeterministic or probabilistic models, several next states may be possible. Interactive and distributed models may additionally depend on messages, external events, timing, or other agents.

The Church–Turing thesis links the intuitive notion of effectively calculable procedures to formal models such as Turing machines, but it is a thesis about the concept of effective computation rather than a theorem derived from one formal system. Modern computer science additionally studies parallel, distributed, probabilistic, analog, and quantum computation, each of which changes which resources or operations are treated as primitive.

== Epistemic notes ==
"Computation" is an established central concept, but its exact boundary depends on context. Theoretical computer science often uses formal models; systems work may focus on concrete execution; natural and physical computation research may use the term more broadly. Claims about what a computational system can or cannot do should therefore name the relevant model and resource assumptions.

